    "body": "In the wake of a successful IPO and strong stock growth throughout the year, Veradermics now has late-stage data to support a regulatory package for its hair loss pill.\nComing off a February IPO that brought in more than $256 million, hair loss biotech Veradermics’ lead asset has shown improved hair coverage in men during a Phase 3 trial.\nVDPHL01, which is an oral extended-release formulation of minoxidil, which showed significant improvement in patient-reported outcomes over placebo.\nThe clinical results cap off a meteoric year for the biotech since the IPO, which was followed by a massive stock surge in the ensuing months. The Connecticut-based drugmaker had been building up to the release of late-stage data for the drug candidate, called VDPHL01, which sets the company up for potential FDA approval.\nThe trial, named Study 304, included more than 500 males with mild-to-moderate pattern hair loss who received either VDPHL01 once or twice a day, or placebo. For one of the two co-primary endpoints measuring hair thickness, patients in the once-daily group had an improvement of 28.6 hairs per square centimeter, while the twice-daily cohort saw an improvement of 36.4 hairs per square centimeter, compared with 6.6 hairs per square centimeter for placebo comparators after six months.\nIPO\nHair loss biotech Veradermics, up 656% since its IPO, credits the obesity boom\nAfter debuting on the public markets with $256.3 million and raking in an additional $472 million , Veradermics has emerged as one of biotech’s biggest post-IPO standouts. CEO Reid Waldman credits the weight loss craze for establishing consumer-driven channels.\nJuly 6, 2026\n·\n5 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more\nOn the second endpoint that measured patient-reported outcomes, 55.7% of men in the once-daily cohort showed “improved” or “much improved” hair coverage based on a rating of alopecia severity, while 68.7% in the subset that received twice-daily medication reported the same. Of patients in the placebo group, 15% reported improvement.\nA secondary measure of investigator perception of hair growth was also statistically significant, with 78.8% of once-daily patients and 82.7% of twice-daily patients showing improvement, compared to 35.3% of patients in the placebo group.\nVeradermics designed its version of minoxidil, also the main ingredient in the household hair loss brand Rogaine, to be delivered in an extended-release formulation using a gel matrix for sustained absorption to improve efficacy and safety, according to the company. The delivery method helps avoid high peak concentrations, allowing more time for hair growth, the company said in a release.\nFor the approximately 50 million men and 30 million women with pattern hair loss, also called androgenetic alopecia, no FDA-approved treatment has entered the U.S. market in almost 30 years, according to Veradermics.\nThe company has not announced a timeline for a regulatory submission but said the data would be used to support an application. The company plans to hold a conference call Monday morning to discuss the results.\nVeradermics CEO Reid Waldman told BioSpace in July that the thriving consumer market behind GLP-1 weight loss drugs from industry giants Eli Lilly and Novo helped convince him and his team that “hair loss could be the next weight loss,” demonstrating that patients would be willing to take certain parts of healthcare into their own hands.\nIn addition to Study 304, Veradermics is running an ongoing Phase 2/3 trial called Study 302 featuring men with pattern hair loss. This study has already shown similar efficacy results and will contribute to the future regulatory submission. Updated results from a second part of Study 302 will be revealed later this year, according to the company.\nThe small drugmaker has also finished enrollment in a Phase 2/3 study for female pattern hair loss and expects to read out results in the first half of next year.\nVeradermics is not the only biotech in the game. Pelage Pharmaceuticals is also racing to market with a novel therapy called PP405 that is currently in Phase 2/3 testing. A Phase 2a read out last year showed that 31% of male patients with a higher degree of hair loss had a greater than 20% increase in hair density at week eight.\nStartups\n‘We’re Scientists’: Pelage Takes Hair Loss Beyond ‘Lotions And Potions’\nA rapturous response to data published last year for Pelage’s hair loss candidate overwhelmed the biotech. Now, the company is ready to show the world the science behind the breakthrough.\nFebruary 11, 2026\n·\n6 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more",
